  "title": {
    "en": "Calls and night migration"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Birds you hear but rarely see: crakes, Quail, Nightjar, Woodcock and the owls"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "A large part of birding is listening. On a spring night you can hear a Corncrake, a Quail and a Spotted Crake from the same field. On an October night, thousands of Redwings, Song Thrushes and Robins pass overhead, calling to each other in the dark, and an experienced birder can tell what is flying over by the calls alone. This lesson trains your ear on birds that are shy, nocturnal or both.\n\nMost of the birds here are not in your photos for a reason: they hide. Water Rail, Corncrake, Spotted Crake and Quail live in thick vegetation. The Nightjar and the owls come out at dusk. The Woodcock is a wader that behaves like an owl. For each, the recording is the main item, and the photo is the reward.\n\nSeason: calls peak in the breeding season, from April to July, but nights in autumn (September to November) are the best for flight calls of migrants. A good way to start is with a nocturnal microphone in the garden."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "roding"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The slow display flight of a male Woodcock over woodland at dusk, calling as he goes."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "churring"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A long, continuous, buzzing trill, as in the song of a Nightjar."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "squeal"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A high, drawn-out cry like a piglet. The Water Rail's call."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "territorial call"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The call that a bird gives to defend its patch, usually heard at dusk or dawn."
      }
    }
  ],
